1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:02,877
The children were asleep.

2
00:00:02,877 --> 00:00:09,027
Fatima al-Rashid had put all three to bed and sat down in the living room.

3
00:00:09,027 --> 00:00:12,766
Since the war began, every sound had changed.

4
00:00:12,766 --> 00:00:15,508
An engine sounded like a missile.

5
00:00:15,508 --> 00:00:18,250
Lightning felt like an explosion.

6
00:00:18,250 --> 00:00:24,981
That night, an Iranian drone struck an eight-story apartment building in Salmiya.

7
00:00:24,981 --> 00:00:26,481
The ceiling shook.

8
00:00:26,481 --> 00:00:31,481
Her youngest was crying — five years old, not even sure why.

9
00:00:31,481 --> 00:00:35,981
Something was frightening, and that was reason enough.

10
00:00:35,981 --> 00:00:39,481
Fatima held her and looked out the window.

11
00:00:39,481 --> 00:00:42,731
Smoke rose from the building next door.

12
00:00:42,731 --> 00:00:44,481
There were no lights.

13
00:00:44,481 --> 00:00:47,231
Wars are fought between soldiers.

14
00:00:47,231 --> 00:00:54,064
But in the darkness a war leaves behind, the ones who cry are always the children.

